---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { articles } from '../lib/content';
const stories=(await articles()).filter(a=>/space|astronomy/i.test(a.category?.title||''));
const films = [
 {title:'Hubble: The Great Space Telescope', id:'Lo43Gq_Xe1M', topic:'Deep space', description:'The telescope’s discoveries and the human work behind its extraordinary view of the universe.', source:'NASA Goddard', url:'https://plus.nasa.gov/video/hubble-the-great-space-telescope/'},
 {title:'The Stuff of Dreams', id:'Qj_62FWfH78', topic:'Voyager', description:'The people and machines behind Voyager’s journey to the outer planets.', source:'NASA JPL', url:'https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/who-we-are/documentary-series-jpl-and-the-space-age/episode-5-the-stuff-of-dreams/'},
 {title:'The Pathfinders', id:'1rVH0YlcKyY', topic:'Mars', description:'How an ambitious landing and a small rover opened a new chapter in exploring Mars.', source:'NASA JPL', url:'https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/who-we-are/documentary-series-jpl-and-the-space-age/episode-8-the-pathfinders/'},
 {title:'Triumph at Saturn — Part I', id:'SY-hQJ5pMd4', topic:'Saturn', description:'Follow the challenges of getting the international Cassini mission to Saturn.', source:'NASA JPL', url:'https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/who-we-are/documentary-series-jpl-and-the-space-age/episode-13-triumph-at-saturn-part-i/'}
];
---
<Layout title="Space & Astronomy | ATHENA" description="Space reporting, documentaries, mission guides and stargazing with ATHENA.">
<div class="space-desk">
<header class="space-hero"><p class="eyebrow">ATHENA / SPACE & ASTRONOMY</p><h1>A wider universe.</h1><p class="standfirst">Discoveries, missions and the people asking what lies beyond our world.</p><nav aria-label="Explore the space desk"><a href="#news">News & articles</a><a href="#live">Live streams</a><a href="#reporting">More reporting</a><a href="#documentaries">Documentary room</a><a href="#explore">Explore missions</a><a href="#skywatching">Stargazing</a></nav></header>

<section id="news"><p class="eyebrow">ATHENA / SPACE NEWS</p><h2>News & articles</h2><div class="space-grid">
<article class="space-card"><p class="eyebrow">MISSION NEWS · 18 NOVEMBER 2026</p><h3>Voyager approaches a distance that takes light a day to cross</h3><p>Voyager 1 is expected to reach one light-day from Earth on 18 November, according to NASA’s advance announcement. That puts the most distant human-made object more than 25.9 billion kilometres away.</p><p>Launched in 1977, the probe entered interstellar space in 2012. Its continuing observations come from beyond the heliosphere, the protective bubble created by the Sun.</p><p>NASA has scheduled a public discussion for 19 November about the two Voyager spacecraft and the challenges of maintaining them. Participants announced include project manager Suzanne Dodd, project scientist Linda Spilker and deputy project scientist Jamie Rankin.</p><p>For students imagining a future in astronomy, Voyager shows how discovery depends on work sustained across generations. This report describes NASA’s announced milestone; it is not independent confirmation that the crossing has occurred.</p><p>The Athena Perspective.</p></article>
<article class="space-card"><p class="eyebrow">ASTRONOMY EXPLAINER · 18 NOVEMBER 2026</p><h3>Webb’s stellar nursery shows why stars do not grow in isolation</h3><p>A Webb image featured by NASA on 16 November shows stars forming inside NGC 7129, where gas, dust and outflows make stellar birth a crowded, changing process.</p><p>Gravity draws material together, while jets and winds from young stars reshape their surroundings. NASA describes the stars in the image’s gold-coloured region as generally more mature than those in the red region.</p><p>The infrared view helps researchers study a setting where dust can obstruct visible light. Comparing stars at different stages reveals clues about the processes that shape their development.</p><p>This is a view of a stellar nursery, not one star’s complete life story. For young readers, it illustrates a central method of astronomy: understanding change by comparing many objects when an entire process is too long to watch unfold.</p><p>The Athena Perspective.</p></article>
</div></section>
<section id="live"><p class="eyebrow">WATCH THE MISSIONS</p><h2>Live streams & upcoming broadcasts</h2><p>Official NASA viewing options. Live availability varies; a player may show a replay or an offline screen between transmissions.</p><div class="space-grid"><article class="space-card"><h3>Space station views</h3><iframe class="space-video" title="NASA space station viewing collection — live availability varies" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/videoseries?list=PL2aBZuCeDwlQMf6xMgQAUAY_nbHAgW5jz" loading="lazy" allow="fullscreen; picture-in-picture; encrypted-media" allowfullscreen></iframe><p>Check the player’s live indicator before treating footage as a current view.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PL2aBZuCeDwlQMf6xMgQAUAY_nbHAgW5jz">Open NASA’s station views →</a></article><article class="space-card"><h3>NASA broadcast desk</h3><p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@NASA/streams">Open NASA live streams →</a></p><p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/live/">Check the official broadcast schedule →</a></p><h3>Voyager mission discussion</h3><p><time datetime="2026-11-19T20:00:00+03:00">19 November 2026 · 8 p.m. Kampala (EAT)</time></p><p>Scheduled discussion with the Voyager team, followed by questions. Times can change; after the event, check NASA’s channel for a replay.</p></article></div></section>
<section id="reporting"><p class="eyebrow">FROM THE NEWSROOM</p><h2>Space reporting</h2>{stories.length>0 ? <div class="space-grid">{stories.map(a=><article class="space-card"><p class="eyebrow">{a.category?.title || 'Space & Astronomy'}</p><h3><a href={'/article/'+a.slug.current+'/'}>{a.title}</a></h3></article>)}</div> : <p>Read the space briefs above, or explore the documentaries and astronomy guides below.</p>}</section>
<section id="documentaries"><p class="eyebrow">WATCH & DISCOVER</p><h2>Documentary room</h2><p>Settle in for stories of exploration from NASA and its Jet Propulsion Laboratory. These archive films provide background, rather than live mission updates.</p><div class="space-grid">{films.map(f=><article class="space-card"><p class="eyebrow">{f.topic} / ARCHIVE FILM</p><iframe class="space-video" title={f.title} src={'https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/'+f.id} loading="lazy" allow="fullscreen; picture-in-picture; encrypted-media" allowfullscreen></iframe><h3>{f.title}</h3><p>{f.description}</p><p class="space-credit">{f.source} · <a href={f.url}>Watch on the official site →</a></p></article>)}</div><div class="space-grid extra-films"><article class="space-card"><p class="eyebrow">WEBB / DOCUMENTARY</p><h3>Cosmic Dawn</h3><p>Go behind the scenes of the James Webb Space Telescope’s assembly, testing and launch.</p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/cosmicdawn/">Watch through NASA →</a></article><article class="space-card"><p class="eyebrow">ROSETTA / DRAMATISED SHORT</p><h3>Ambition</h3><p>ESA and Platige Image bring the spirit of comet exploration to a science-fiction short. A creative companion to the science, rather than a documentary.</p><a href="https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Videos/2014/10/Ambition_the_film">Watch through ESA →</a></article></div></section>
<section id="explore"><p class="eyebrow">GO FURTHER</p><h2>Your mission guide</h2><div class="space-grid"><article class="space-card"><h3>Explore the solar system in 3D</h3><p>Open NASA’s Eyes visualisations to explore space through interactive views.</p><a href="https://eyes.nasa.gov/">Explore NASA’s Eyes →</a></article><article class="space-card"><h3>A whole history of exploration</h3><p>Continue through JPL and the Space Age, a documentary collection about the people behind robotic exploration.</p><a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/who-we-are/documentary-series-jpl-and-the-space-age/">Browse the full collection →</a></article></div></section>
<section id="skywatching" class="sky-panel"><p class="eyebrow">LOOK UP / KAMPALA & BEYOND</p><h2>Your first night under the stars</h2><p>You can begin without a telescope. Choose a safe spot away from bright lights, check cloud cover, and give your eyes time to adjust to the dark.</p><div class="space-grid"><article><h3>Start with what you can see</h3><p>Follow the Moon over several evenings. A pair of binoculars can be a useful next step once you know your way around the sky. Never point binoculars or a telescope at the Sun without appropriate solar equipment.</p></article><article><h3>Make the guide local</h3><p>Set any sky chart to Kampala or your actual location and local time. Visibility depends on your horizon, weather and location; an overseas viewing time may not apply.</p></article></div><p><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/skywatching/whats-up/">NASA’s monthly skywatching guide →</a></p><p><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/skywatching/tips-guides/">Beginner tips and observing guides →</a></p></section>
</div>
</Layout>
<style>
.space-desk{max-width:1160px;margin:auto;padding:28px 20px 64px}.space-hero{border-top:5px solid #a91935;border-bottom:1px solid #ccc;padding:30px 0}.space-hero h1{font-size:clamp(2.6rem,6vw,4.8rem);line-height:1.06;margin:14px 0}.space-hero nav{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:12px 24px;margin-top:25px}.space-hero nav a{font-weight:700;color:#a91935}.space-desk section{margin-top:48px;scroll-margin-top:24px}.space-desk h2{font-size:2rem;margin:8px 0 18px}.space-desk h3{font-size:1.35rem;line-height:1.3}.space-desk p{line-height:1.65}.space-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:24px}.space-card{border:1px solid #ddd;padding:22px;background:#fff;color:#171717}.space-card h3 a{color:inherit}.space-video{display:block;width:100%;aspect-ratio:16/9;border:0;background:#111}.space-credit{font-size:.85rem}.extra-films{margin-top:24px}.sky-panel{background:#f7f3f4;border-top:4px solid #a91935;padding:28px;color:#171717}.space-desk a:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #a91935;outline-offset:4px}@media(max-width:640px){.space-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.space-card{padding:16px}.space-desk{padding-inline:16px}.sky-panel{padding:20px}}
</style>
